<meta type=article title="Deferred-vocab sub-slice 2 fixture">
</meta>

<section | Definition list>

A `<dl>` pairs terms with their descriptions:

<dl>
<dt | acadamark>
<dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
<dt | Layer 1>
<dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
<dt | Layer 2>
<dd | The shorthand authoring syntax that compiles to Layer 1.>
</dl>

<section | Glossary>

A `<glossary>` is a curated collection of `<glossary-entry>` items,
each carrying a `<dt>`/`<dd>` pair:

<glossary #project-terms>
<glossary-entry #term:acadamark>
<dt | acadamark>
<dd | The publishing system.>
</glossary-entry>
<glossary-entry #term:layer1>
<dt | Layer 1>
<dd | The canonical vocabulary.>
</glossary-entry>
</glossary>

<section | Collapsible disclosure>

A `<details>` block carries a `<summary>` heading; the body shows
when the disclosure is opened:

<details>
<summary | Background reading>
Additional context for the curious reader. The summary above is the
visible heading; this paragraph is the disclosure body.
</details>

A second `<details>` with an `open` kwarg expands by default:

<details open=true>
<summary | Always-expanded note>
This disclosure is expanded by default.
</details>

This fixture exercises all seven sub-slice-2 elements end-to-end. The
test asserts each renders as a real custom (or HTML-native) element
with its content — no `data-acadamark-unknown` spans remain. Closed-
child-set / parent-child structural validation is a spec concern only;
the parser does not enforce it (always-renders posture).

(Note on the `open` form: the kwarg form `open=true` is used here
rather than the `+open` boolean form because of a pre-existing
limitation in `buildProperties` — it does not iterate `node.booleans`,
so `+flag` markers silently drop through the schema dispatch. The
limitation is filed as a `[post-alpha]` backlog item for a future
fix; this slice does not address it. Once fixed, `+open` will render
identically to `open=true`.)
